From: Overalls maintainers
Subject: [PATCH] Restore File-backend snapshots into anonymous guest memory

Upstream maps the snapshot memory file MAP_PRIVATE as guest RAM. Every page
the guest or VMM reads is then a page-cache folio of that file charged to the
VMM's memory cgroup, beside the anonymous copy of every page the guest writes.
The next full snapshot of the restored guest writes guest RAM to a new file;
the kernel prefaults the write source and faults the whole file mapping into
the cgroup, and the mapped, recently referenced folios are not reclaimed before
the limit. A 512 MiB guest restored on ZFS reached a 576 MiB MemoryMax that
way and, with panic_on_oom, panicked its provisioner host (ADR-0266).

Allocate anonymous private memory as a cold boot does and copy the file into
it before KVM registration and before the VMM seccomp filter is installed.
All-zero host pages are not written and stay unpopulated. A short file is still
refused; dirty tracking starts clean. The API, snapshot format and version are
unchanged; only the residency of the restored memory changes.

